     1	# OrbitRIR: Frame-Averaged Few-Shot RIR Synthesis for 3D Sound Source Localization
     2	
     3	This repository includes:
     4	- **FLAC**: the base model for one-shot room impulse response generation, extended here with frame-averaged (yaw-equivariant) conditioning.
     5	- **AGREE**: a CLIP-style acoustic–geometry embedding model introduced for evaluating geometry-consistenty in RIR generation and enabling downstream tasks. 
     6	
     7	
     8	## Method Overview
     9	
    10	![Overview](assets/fa_cond.png)
    11	
    12	## Install
    13	Clone this repository, navigate to the root, and run:
    14	```bash
    15	conda create -n orbitrir python=3.10
    16	conda activate orbitrir
    17	pip install .
    18	```
    19	
    20	**Hardware Requirements**
    21	- Evaluation: All evaluation can be performed on an RTX 4090 (24GB). Frame-averaged training fits two 48GB GPUs (see the dedicated section).
    22	- FLAC Training: Training the VAE and the main FLAC model with EMA requires an H100 (80GB).
    23	- Flash Attention: Requires PyTorch >= 2.5. We use version 2.7.4.post1. Refer to the [Flash Attention GitHub repo](https://github.com/Dao-AILab/flash-attention) for troubleshooting.
    24	
    25	
    26	## Quick Start: Inference
    27	To quickly generate RIRs and evaluate FLAC using pretrained weights
    28	1. Download weights: run `bash download_weights.sh` in the repo root.
    29	2. Download the [AcousticRooms dataset](https://github.com/facebookresearch/AcousticRooms).
    30	3. Run:
    31	```bash
    32	python eval_FLAC.py \
    33	--model-config src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR.json \
    34	--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/eval/acousticroom_seeneval.json \
    35	--ckpt-path weights/FLAC/FLAC_EMA.ckpt \
    36	--cfg-scale 1.0 \
    37	--steps 1 \
    38	--eval-name eval_FLAC \
    39	--store_predictions
    40	```
    41	
    42	## Global Repository Structure
    43	```
    44	.
    45	├── AGREE/               # AGREE model & training (Run AGREE commands from here)
    46	├── assets/              # README images
    47	├── baselines/           # Baseline heuristics (KNN, etc.)
    48	├── data/                # Dataset JSON splits
    49	├── src/                 # Core FLAC code
    50	│   ├── configs/         # All .json configurations
    51	│   ├── ...
    52	├── weights/             # Model checkpoints (Download here)
    53	├── download_weights.sh  # Script to download weights from Hugging Face
    54	└── *.py                 # Main entry points (train.py, eval_*.py)
    55	```
    56	
    57	## Datasets Preparation
    58	
    59	### AcousticRooms (AR)
    60	Instructions for downloading the dataset are available [here](https://github.com/facebookresearch/AcousticRooms).
    61	
    62	Unlike the original xRIR setup which trains separate models for evaluation on seen/unseen sets, we train a single model for evaluation on both. We modified the original split to ensure zero overlap between the training set and evaluation sets. Use the updated files in `data/AR/`.
    63	
    64	
    65	## Weights
    66	The weights for FLAC and AGREE can be downloaded from Hugging Face by running `download_weights.sh` in the repo root. Place the downloaded files in the `weights/` folder.
    67	
    68	
    69	### Model Unwrapping
    70	The checkpoint files created during training include the training wrapper, which greatly increases the size of the checkpoint file.
    71	`unwrap_model.py` takes in a wrapped model checkpoint and saves a new checkpoint file including only the model itself.
    72	
    73	That can be run with from the repo root with:
    74	```bash
    75	python unwrap_model.py \
    76	--model-config /path/to/model/config \
    77	--ckpt-path /path/to/wrapped/ckpt \
    78	--name model_unwrap \
    79	--use-safetensors
    80	```
    81	
    82	## Training 
    83	
    84	### FLAC
    85	To start a training run on the AcousticRooms dataset, run the `train.py` script in the repo root with:
    86	```bash
    87	python train.py \
    88	--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/train/acousticroom_train.json \
    89	--val-dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/eval/acousticroom_seeneval.json \
    90	--val-every 2500 \
    91	--checkpoint-every 2500 \
    92	--batch-size 64 \
    93	--num-workers 8 \
    94	--model-config src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR.json \
    95	--pretransform-ckpt-path weights/FLAC/VAE.safetensors \
    96	--save-dir ./outputs_FLAC \
    97	--experiment-name FLAC_training \
    98	--name FLAC
    99	```
   100	
   101	### VAE
   102	The VAE takes multiple days on an H100. We provide a pretrained checkpoint (3.08M steps). To train from scratch:
   103	```bash
   104	python train.py \
   105	--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/train/acousticroom_train.json \
   106	--val-dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/eval/acousticroom_seeneval.json \
   107	--model-config src/configs/model_configs/VAE/VAE.json \
   108	--batch-size 64 \
   109	--num-workers 8 \
   110	--val-every 10000 \
   111	--save-dir ./outputs_VAE \
   112	--name VAE \
   113	--experiment-name VAE_training
   114	```
   115	**Note**: Trained VAE checkpoints must be processed via `unwrap_model.py` before use in FLAC training.
   116	
   117	**Paper Metrics**: To obtain the metrics presented in the paper, we averaged over individual results per scene. The evaluation script also provides a mean over all the samples from all the scenes, which is different.
   118	
   119	### FLAC Config Variants
   120	We provide the configs for the FLAC model variants.
   121	| Config File | Conditioning | Description |
   122	| ----------- | ------------ | ----------- |
   123	| `FLAC_AR.json` | AdaLN + Cross-Attn | Baseline. |
   124	| `FLAC_AR_S.json` | AdaLN + Cross-Attn | Smaller DiT (Depth of 4). Similar performances. |
   125	| `FLAC_AR_FA.json` | AdaLN + Cross-Attn | Frame-averaged (yaw-equivariant) conditioning; see the dedicated section below. |
   126	| `FLAC_AR_AllCA.json` | Cross-Attn| All conditioning via cross-attention. |
   127	| `FLAC_AR_InContext.json` | Prepended | Conditioning prepended to input sequence. |
   128	| `FLAC_AR_VAECtxt.json` | AdaLN + Cross-Attn | Uses VAE to encode context RIRs instead of ResNet18. Works with dataset config `acousticroom_train_VAECtxt.json`|
   129	| `FLAC_AR_noGeom.json` | AdaLN + Cross-Attn | Evaluates FLAC without geometric conditioning (ablation).|
   130	
   131	
   132	## Frame-Averaged (Yaw-Equivariant) Conditioning
   133	
   134	For a mono RIR, rigidly rotating a whole scene -- geometry, source and listener together -- about
   135	the vertical axis leaves the ground-truth response unchanged, yet the standard conditioning is not
   136	invariant to that rotation: the absolute pose vectors and the equirectangular depth panorama both
   137	move. Setting `training.cond_method` to `"fa_invariant"` makes the conditioning exactly invariant
   138	along two independent paths. The source and context poses become cylindrical features
   139	`(r, z, dphi)`, where `dphi` is a pose's azimuth measured *relative* to the target source's
   140	azimuth; a rigid yaw shifts every azimuth by the same amount, so these features are invariant at
   141	**any** angle. The two DINOv3 depth-panorama conditioners (`source_vit`, `context_poses_vit`) are
   142	instead **frame-averaged** over the C4 orbit: per angle the depth map is rolled horizontally and
   143	the `*_vit` poses are rotated *together*, and the conditioner outputs are averaged. At the panorama
   144	width used here (512 columns) 90 degrees is exactly 128 columns, so the roll is a lossless integer
   145	shift and the average is exactly invariant on that subgroup. All other conditioners run once.
   146	
   147	The configuration is `src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR_FA.json`: the baseline
   148	`FLAC_AR.json` plus exactly four keys -- `training.cond_method`, `training.frame_avg_angles`, and
   149	`gradient_checkpointing` on each of the two ViT conditioners. Both configs pin the DINOv3 backbone
   150	to an immutable Hub snapshot (`revision`). The orbit is configurable through
   151	`training.frame_avg_angles` (degrees): the first angle must be `0` (the identity pass), and angles
   152	whose column shift (`angle / 360 * 512`) is an integer -- as it is for the default `[0, 90, 180, 270]` -- are exact;
   153	any other angle is quantised to the nearest column.
   154	
   155	### Training
   156	
   157	```bash
   158	python train.py \
   159	--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/train/acousticroom_train.json \
   160	--model-config src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR_FA.json \
   161	--pretransform-ckpt-path weights/FLAC/VAE.safetensors \
   162	--max-steps 67500 --batch-size 32 --accum-batches 1 --seed 42 \
   163	--num-gpus 2 --strategy ddp_find_unused_parameters_true --sync-batchnorm true \
   164	--checkpoint-every 2500 \
   165	--name FLAC_FA --experiment-name FA_training --save-dir ./outputs_FLAC
   166	```
   167	
   168	`--sync-batchnorm true` synchronises BatchNorm statistics across ranks, so BatchNorm aggregates
   169	the full global scene batch of 32 x 2 = 64 (the context-audio encoder additionally folds its K
   170	reference RIRs into the BatchNorm input); it requires `--num-gpus >= 2` and fails closed otherwise,
   171	and gradient accumulation is no substitute (BatchNorm only ever sees one micro-batch).
   172	`gradient_checkpointing` in the config trades backward-pass recompute for the activation memory the
   173	four-angle orbit costs, which is what lets a micro-batch of 32 fit on a 48 GB card. Because the ViT
   174	conditioners are re-run per orbit angle, each optimizer step is correspondingly slower than the
   175	standard recipe.
   176	
   177	### Evaluation
   178	
   179	```bash
   180	python eval_FLAC.py \
   181	--model-config src/configs/model_configs/FLAC/AR/FLAC_AR_FA.json \
   182	--dataset-config src/configs/dataset_configs/AR/eval/acousticroom_unseeneval.json \
   183	--ckpt-path /path/to/FLAC_FA/ckpt \
   184	--cond-autocast bf16 --steps 1 --cfg-scale 1.0 --seed 42
   185	```
   186	
   187	`--cond-method` **defaults to inheriting the method the checkpoint was trained with**, read from the
   188	`training` block embedded in the checkpoint (or, for a bare exported checkpoint, from
   189	`--model-config`); `--frame-avg-angles` inherits the trained orbit the same way. A value that
   190	contradicts the trained protocol is refused unless `--allow-conditioning-override` is passed, and an
   191	overriding run is marked as such in its output record. The guard exists because the failure mode is
   192	silent: evaluating frame-averaged weights under vanilla conditioning -- or vanilla weights under
   193	frame-averaged conditioning -- produces plausible-looking numbers, wrong in both directions.
   194	
   195	`--cond-autocast bf16` runs the conditioning call in bfloat16, matching the `bf16-mixed` precision
   196	the model trains under. The CLI default is `default` (the per-device autocast dtype, i.e. fp16 on
   197	CUDA) and the choice measurably shifts the metrics, so comparable runs must all pass it explicitly.
   198	
   199	### Rotation-robustness check
   200	
   201	`--rotate-deg 90` physically rotates the conditioning -- depth panorama and poses together -- before
   202	it is encoded, while the target RIR stays fixed; the rotation is applied first and the conditioning
   203	method on top of it. An `fa_invariant` model should return unchanged metrics at the C4 angles,
   204	because its conditioning is invariant there by construction. Output files pick up a `_rot<deg>`
   205	suffix (alongside a `_fa_invariant_a<n>` method suffix), so the runs of a sweep never overwrite
   206	each other.
   207	
   208	### Supported inference API
   209	
   210	`train.py`, `eval_FLAC.py` and `eval_pl.py` all build their conditioning through one shared dispatch
   211	function, `src.data.yaw_rotation.dispatch_conditioning`, so a checkpoint's method is honoured
   212	identically wherever it is run. The lower-level `generate_diffusion_cond` /
   213	`ConditionedDiffusionModelWrapper.generate()` path does **not** go through that dispatch: given raw
   214	`conditioning` it calls the conditioner directly, which is the vanilla path. To drive a
   215	frame-averaged model through it, precompute the conditioning and pass it as tensors instead:
   216	
   217	```python
   218	from src.data.yaw_rotation import invariant_conditioning
   219	
   220	cond = invariant_conditioning(model.conditioner, metadata, device)
   221	audio = model.generate(steps=1, cfg_scale=1.0, conditioning_tensors=cond, sample_size=10240,
   222	                       batch_size=len(metadata), device=device)
   223	```
   224	
   225	
   226	## Evaluation
   227	
   228	### FLAC
   229	Evaluate using either the standard script or the PyTorch Lightning wrapper:
   230	
   231	```bash
   232	# Standard script
   233	python eval_FLAC.py \
   234	--model-config /path/to/model/config \
   235	--dataset-config /path/to/eval/dataset/config \
   236	--cfg-scale 1.0 \
   237	--steps 1 \
   238	--eval-name eval_FLAC \
